om_idx] for free() and swap-remove with msg[cookie_num-1]\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"Index is random (not attacker-controlled), but the swap-remove pattern (msg[random_idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]) combined with free() of the selected pointer creates a potential UAF/double-free scenario if the same pointer appears at multiple indices. The freed pointer is overwritten by the last element, but if random_idx == cookie_num-1, the pointer is freed then the same slot is written with the freed pointer value.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_size (scanf %lld)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(cookie_size, 1) then read(0, buf, cookie_size)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"cookie_size is checked <= 0x100 (256) with jle (signed). calloc and read use the same size. No obvious overflow.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is ELF64 PIE, dynamically linked, not stripped, compiled with GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Security: Full RELRO, NX enabled, Canary found, PIE enabled\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8 at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[32] (char* array) at 0x4060-0x415f (256 bytes), msg_size[32] (int32 array) at 0x4160-0x41df (128 bytes), urandom (FILE*) at 0x41e0, cookie_num (int64) at 0x41e8\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-populates 5 cookies: msg[0..4] via strdup, msg_size[0..4] = {0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40}, cookie_num = 5\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num <= 0x1f (max 32 entries) and cookie_size <= 0x100 (max 256 bytes), uses calloc(cookie_size,1) then read(0,buf,cookie_size)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie reads 1 random byte from /dev/urandom, computes random_index = random_byte % cookie_num, prints msg[random_index], frees msg[random_index], then does swap-remove: msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[random_index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads cookie_index via scanf(%lld), checks cookie_index < cookie_num using signed jl (no lower bound check), then reads msg_size[cookie_index]-1 bytes via read(0, msg[cookie_index], msg_size[cookie_index]-1)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads cookie_index via scanf(%lld), checks cookie_index < cookie_num using signed jl (no lower bound check), then prints msg[cookie_index] via printf(%s)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie both use signed comparison jl (jump if less) for index check: cmp rdx, rax; jl. This means negative indices pass the check since negative < cookie_num in signed comparison\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060, 32 elements of 8 bytes) and msg_size array (0x4160, 32 elements of 4 bytes) are adjacent in BSS. msg_size starts exactly at msg+0x100\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num at 0x41e8 is 8 bytes after urandom at 0x41e0, both after msg_size array end at 0x41df\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Runtime confirmed: program shows menu with 5 options, initial cookie_num=5, view cookie shows fortune messages, prompt format is 'Which cookie?[0-N]: '\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"main loop: calls init() once, then loops menu() -> scanf(%lld) -> dispatch to eat/create/edit/read/exit, loops back to menu() after each action except exit\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) called in init(), setting 60-second timeout\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58, size 0xa8 (13 entries), writable but Full RELRO means it is made read-only after relocation\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"Negative index in edit_cookie/read_cookie can reach addresses before msg[0] (0x4060). For example, index -1 reaches msg[-1] at 0x4058 (completed.0 area), index -3 reaches 0x4048, etc. The msg_size array at 0x4160 can also be reached with positive out-of-bounds indices beyond cookie_num if cookie_num < 32.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, when random_index == cookie_num-1 (the last element), the sequence is: free(msg[cookie_num-1]) then msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment of freed pointer), msg_size[cookie_num-1] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment), cookie_num--. The freed pointer remains in the msg array at the same index (now cookie_num-1 after decrement, i.e. still accessible if new cookies are created).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether negative index in edit_cookie can reach msg_size array or other exploitable data. msg[-1] = 0x4058 which is in completed.0/padding area. Need to verify what values are at negative offsets and whether they form valid pointers.\",\"reason\":\"Static analysis shows the indexing is possible but the content at negative offsets needs runtime verification\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's swap-remove creates a exploitable UAF when random_index == cookie_num-1. The freed pointer is self-assigned back, but cookie_num is decremented making it 'out of range' for edit/read. However create_cookie would reuse that slot.\",\"reason\":\"Need S2 to analyze the control flow and determine if the freed pointer can be accessed again through create_cookie reusing the slot\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size values at negative offsets (if reachable via negative index in edit_cookie) could provide a large write size. msg_size[-1] at 0x415c would read 4 bytes from the end of msg array (which contains heap pointers).\",\"reason\":\"The msg array contains heap pointers from strdup/calloc; interpreting the low 4 bytes of a heap pointer as msg_size could yield a large value, enabling heap overflow via read()\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"ASLR status cannot be confirmed from static analysis. PIE is enabled but ASLR depends on kernel configuration.\",\"reason\":\"ASLR is a runtime OS feature, not determinable from ELF alone\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"FORTIFY_SOURCE status unclear - no __chk symbols observed but cannot definitively confirm absence\",\"reason\":\"checksec does not report FORTIFY, and no __*_chk symbols in PLT, but absence of evidence is not definitive\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0, entry=0x1110\",\"sections\":\"29 sections: .text@0x1110(RX,0x891), .rodata@0x2000(R,0x266), .data@0x4000(RW,0x10), .bss@0x4020(RW,0x1d0), .got@0x3f58(RW,0xa8), .plt@0x1020(RX,0xe0)\",\"symbols\":\"Key: _abort@0x11f5, init@0x1232, create_cookie@0x1399, eat_cookie@0x14e1, edit_cookie@0x1628, read_cookie@0x1751, menu@0x17fd, main@0x187f. Globals: msg[32]@0x4060(256B), msg_size[32]@0x4160(128B), urandom@0x41e0, cookie_num@0x41e8\",\"disassembly\":\"edit_cookie: scanf(%lld)->cookie_index, cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl (signed, no lower bound), read(0, msg[cookie_index], msg_size[cookie_index]-1). read_cookie: scanf(%lld)->cookie_index, cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl (signed, no lower bound), printf(%s, msg[cookie_index]). eat_cookie: fread(urandom,1,1)->random_byte, idx=random_byte%cookie_num, printf(%s,msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--. create_cookie: scanf(%lld)->size, check size<=0x100, calloc(size,1), read(0,buf,size), msg[cookie_num]=buf, msg_size[cookie_num]=size, cookie_num++\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":2,\"high_severity\":1,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_INDEX_CHECK\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read cookie_index as a signed int64 via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and validate it against cookie_num using a signed comparison (jl / jump-if-less). Since jl performs signed comparison, any negative index satisfies the condition (negative < positive in signed arithmetic). There is no lower-bound check (no test for index >= 0). This allows attacker-controlled negative indices to access memory before the msg[] array in BSS, reaching global FILE* pointers (stdout, stdin, stderr) stored at lower addresses.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[32] (char* array at BSS 0x4060)\",\"msg_size[32] (int32 array at BSS 0x4160)\",\"stdout (FILE* at BSS 0x4020)\",\"stdin (FILE* at BSS 0x4030)\",\"stderr (FILE* at BSS 0x4040)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") in edit_cookie and read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu option 3 or 4)\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker sends menu choice 3 (edit) or 4 (read)\",\"scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") reads cookie_index as signed int64 into stack variable\",\"cookie_index compared to cookie_num using 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed less-than)\",\"Negative index passes check because negative < cookie_num in signed comparison\",\"edit_cookie: msg_size[cookie_index] is read (4 bytes from BSS at 0x4160 + idx*4), decremented by 1, sign-extended to 64-bit via movsxd, used as size argument to read()\",\"edit_cookie: msg[cookie_index] is read (8 bytes from BSS at 0x4060 + idx*8), used as buffer pointer argument to read(0, buf, size)\",\"read_cookie: msg[cookie_index] is read (8 bytes from BSS at 0x4060 + idx*8), passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\") as string argument\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated with both lower bound (>= 0) and upper bound (< cookie_num) using unsigned comparison\",\"actual\":\"Only upper bound check via signed jl (jump-if-less): cmp rdx, rax; jl. No lower bound check. Negative values pass because signed comparison treats them as less than cookie_num.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). The signed comparison jl allows negative indices to bypass the boundary check.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access and pointer dereference\",\"address\":\"edit_cookie: 0x16a7 (msg_size[idx] read), 0x16c3 (msg[idx] read), 0x16de (read() call); read_cookie: 0x17bb (msg[idx] read), 0x17e1 (printf call)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[cookie_index], msg_size[cookie_index]-1); read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[cookie_index])\",\"target\":\"msg[cookie_index] dereferences a pointer read from BSS at address 0x4060 + cookie_index*8. For negative indices, this reads FILE* pointers (stdout/stdin/stderr) stored at lower BSS addresses.\",\"size\":\"edit_cookie: msg_size[cookie_index]-1 bytes. For negative indices where msg_size is 0, size becomes 0-1=-1, sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (effectively unlimited). read_cookie: unbounded string read via printf %s.\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie with index -4, msg[-4] reads the stderr FILE* pointer (0x4020+0x20=0x4040, value=libc FILE struct address). msg_size[-4] reads 0 from BSS padding. read(0, FILE_struct_ptr, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) writes attacker-controlled bytes from stdin directly into the FILE structure in libc writable memory. GDB confirmed: rsi=0x7ffff7fa74e0 (stderr FILE struct), rdx=0xffffffffffffffff. The program did not crash, confirming successful write to FILE structure. Similarly, index -6 targets stdin FILE* and index -8 targets stdout FILE*.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie with index -4, msg[-4] reads the stderr FILE* pointer. printf(\\\"%s\\\", FILE_struct_ptr) prints bytes from the FILE structure until a NUL byte is encountered, leaking libc internal memory contents including libc addresses. Runtime confirmed: output contained bytes 0x87 0x20 0xad 0xfb from the FILE structure, which are libc address bytes.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie with index -1 or -2, msg[-1]/msg[-2] reads 0x0000000000000000 (NULL pointer from BSS padding). read(0, NULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) causes SIGSEGV. GDB confirmed SIGSEGV at edit_cookie+0xd7 when attempting to dereference NULL.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Index check: 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 1692' uses signed comparison jl (jump-if-less). cookie_index (rdx) is from scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") as signed int64. No lower-bound check exists. Negative values pass because -N < cookie_num in signed arithmetic.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Index check: 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 17bb' uses signed comparison jl. Same signedness mismatch as edit_cookie. No lower-bound check.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16de\",\"description\":\"Data flow: msg_size[cookie_index] read at 0x16a7 (lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,msg_size; mov eax,[rdx+rax]; sub eax,1; movsxd rdx,eax), then msg[cookie_index] read at 0x16c3 (lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,msg; mov rax,[rcx+rax]), then read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) at 0x16de. Negative index accesses BSS before msg array.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at read() call with index=-4: rsi=0x7ffff7fa74e0 (stderr FILE struct address in libc), rdx=0xffffffffffffffff (size from msg_size[-4]=0, 0-1=-1, sign-extended). Program did not crash, confirming write to FILE structure succeeded.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"Runtime output with index=-4: printf(\\\"%s\\\", stderr_FILE_ptr) printed bytes 0x87 0x20 0xad 0xfb from the FILE structure, confirming information leak of libc memory contents.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060-0x4020\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[32] at 0x4060, stdout at 0x4020, stdin at 0x4030, stderr at 0x4040. msg[-4] = *(0x4060-32) = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE*. msg[-6] = *(0x4030) = stdin FILE*. msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*. These are valid libc pointers that can be dereferenced.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed msg_size[-4] at 0x4150 = 0x00000000 (BSS padding). Therefore read size = 0-1 = -1, sign-extended via movsxd to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, passed as size_t to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1705\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed SIGSEGV at 0x1705 (edit_cookie+0xd7) with index=-3: msg[-3] at 0x4048 = 0x0000000000000000 (NULL). read(0, NULL, huge) causes NULL pointer dereference.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Determine exact FILE structure fields overwritten by read() at offset 0 of stderr/stdin/stdout FILE structures and their security implications\",\"Verify whether the write to FILE structure can corrupt function pointers or vtable pointers within the FILE structure\",\"Assess whether the info leak from read_cookie can reliably disclose libc base address for ASLR bypass\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"HEAP_USE_AFTER_FREE_VIA_SWAP_REMOVE_SELF_ASSIGNMENT\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie implements a swap-remove pattern: it frees msg[random_index], then assigns msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1] and msg_size[random_index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1], then decrements cookie_num. When random_index == cookie_num-1 (the last element), the freed pointer is self-assigned back to the same slot (msg[idx] = msg[idx]), leaving a dangling pointer in the msg array. After cookie_num--, this slot is at index cookie_num (new value), which is out of bounds for edit_cookie/read_cookie (they check idx < cookie_num). However, create_cookie writes to msg[cookie_num], overwriting the dangling pointer. Additionally, when random_index != cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is overwritten by msg[cookie_num-1]'s value, but msg[cookie_num-1] retains its original value, creating a duplicate pointer at two indices. If the duplicate is later freed via eat_cookie selecting the other copy, a double-free occurs.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[32] (char* array at BSS 0x4060)\",\"msg_size[32] (int32 array at BSS 0x4160)\",\"cookie_num (int64 at BSS 0x41e8)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_byte from /dev/urandom via fread (not directly attacker-controlled)\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu option 1)\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie reads 1 random byte from /dev/urandom\",\"Computes random_index = random_byte % cookie_num\",\"Prints msg[random_index] via printf\",\"Frees msg[random_index] via free()\",\"Performs swap-remove: msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[random_index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1]\",\"Decrements cookie_num\",\"When random_index == cookie_num-1: freed pointer is self-assigned back, dangling pointer remains in msg array at index that becomes out-of-bounds after decrement\",\"When random_index != cookie_num-1: freed pointer is overwritten, but msg[cookie_num-1] still holds same value as msg[random_index] (duplicate pointer)\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After freeing msg[random_index], the freed pointer should be cleared (set to NULL) and the slot should not retain a dangling reference. The swap-remove should not create duplicate pointers.\",\"actual\":\"No NULL-ification of freed pointer. Self-assignment when random_index == cookie_num-1 leaves dangling pointer. Swap-remove creates duplicate pointers when random_index != cookie_num-1.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing NULL-ification of freed pointer. Missing check for random_index == cookie_num-1 to avoid self-assignment of freed pointer.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"free(msg[random_index]) followed by dangling pointer retention\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free call), 0x15b4-0x15c6 (swap-assign msg), 0x15e7-0x15fc (swap-assign msg_size), 0x160a (cookie_num--)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1]; cookie_num--;\",\"target\":\"msg[random_index] heap pointer\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"When random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed heap pointer remains in msg[cookie_num-1] (which becomes msg[cookie_num] after decrement). create_cookie will reuse this slot (msg[cookie_num] = new_ptr), overwriting the dangling pointer. If the freed chunk is reallocated and the new allocation is used via edit_cookie before the slot is overwritten, a use-after-free write could corrupt the new allocation's data. However, the slot is at index cookie_num (out of bounds for edit/read), so direct access requires create_cookie to overwrite it first, which eliminates the dangling pointer.\"},{\"type\":\"DATA_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"When random_index != cookie_num-1, the swap-remove creates a duplicate pointer: msg[random_index] and msg[cookie_num-1] both point to the same heap block (the one originally at msg[cookie_num-1]). The freed block (originally at msg[random_index]) is gone. If eat_cookie later selects msg[cookie_num-1] (now at a different index after decrements) and frees it, while msg[random_index] still holds the same pointer, a double-free occurs. This is probabilistic because the index is random.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e-0x160a\",\"description\":\"Swap-remove sequence: free(msg[idx]) at 0x158e, then msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15b4-0x15c6, msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15e7-0x15fc, cookie_num-- at 0x160a. No NULL-ification of freed pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1626\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed: when idx=4 and cookie_num=5 (idx == cookie_num-1), after free(msg[4]) and self-assignment, msg[4] still contains the freed pointer 0x555555559580. cookie_num becomes 4, making index 4 out of bounds for edit/read but accessible by create_cookie.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1626\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed: when idx=0 and cookie_num=5, after free(msg[0]) and swap-remove (msg[0]=msg[4]=0x555555559540), both msg[0] and msg[3] contain 0x555555559540 (duplicate pointer). cookie_num=4, so both indices 0 and 3 are accessible (0<4, 3<4).\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Verify whether the dangling pointer at msg[cookie_num] after self-assignment can be accessed before create_cookie overwrites it (e.g., via negative index from BUG-001)\",\"Determine probability of double-free via duplicate pointers given random index selection\",\"Assess whether the 60-second alarm timeout limits the feasibility of probabilistic UAF exploitation\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_EDIT_SIZE_CALCULATION\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, the write size for read() is computed as msg_size[cookie_index] - 1. msg_size is an int32 array. When msg_size[cookie_index] is 0 (which occurs at negative indices where BSS padding is zero, or at uninitialized positive indices), the subtraction 0 - 1 = -1 (int32). This value is then sign-extended to int64 via 'movsxd rdx, eax', producing 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. This is passed as the size_t (unsigned) third argument to read(), resulting in an effectively unlimited read size.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size[32] (int32 array at BSS 0x4160)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") in edit_cookie (combined with BUG-001 negative index)\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu option 3)\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker sends menu choice 3 (edit)\",\"scanf reads negative cookie_index (e.g., -4)\",\"msg_size[cookie_index] read from BSS: for negative indices, reads 0 from BSS padding\",\"msg_size[cookie_index] - 1 = 0 - 1 = -1 (int32 0xFFFFFFFF)\",\"movsxd sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (int64)\",\"read(0, msg[cookie_index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) called with unlimited size\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Size should be validated to be > 0 before subtraction, or unsigned arithmetic should be used with a check for zero.\",\"actual\":\"No validation of msg_size[cookie_index] before subtraction. Direct subtraction of 1 from potentially zero value, followed by signed sign-extension to 64-bit.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing check for msg_size[cookie_index] == 0 before computing size = msg_size[cookie_index] - 1. Missing unsigned conversion to prevent sign-extension of negative result.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"read() with unlimited size\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] read at 0x16b6 (mov eax, [rdx+rax]), sub eax,1 at 0x16b9, movsxd rdx,eax at 0x16bc, read(0, buf, rdx) at 0x16de\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[cookie_index], (int64)(msg_size[cookie_index] - 1))\",\"target\":\"Heap buffer or FILE structure pointed by msg[cookie_index]\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (effectively unlimited) when msg_size[cookie_index] == 0\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"When combined with BUG-001 (negative index), the unlimited read size allows writing an arbitrary number of attacker-controlled bytes to the target buffer. For index -4 (stderr FILE*), this writes unlimited bytes to the FILE structure in libc. For valid heap indices where msg_size is correctly set, the size is bounded. The underflow only occurs when msg_size[idx] is 0, which happens at negative indices or uninitialized slots.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16bc\",\"description\":\"Size computation: 'mov eax, [rdx+rax]' (read msg_size[idx] as int32), 'sub eax, 1' (subtract 1, underflows if 0), 'movsxd rdx, eax' (sign-extend int32 to int64, producing 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF for -1).\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB confirmed: with index=-4, rdx (size argument to read) = 0xffffffffffffffff. msg_size[-4] was 0x00000000, so 0-1=-1, sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"This bug is a contributing factor to BUG-001's MEMORY_CORRUPTION impact. It amplifies the write capability when combined with the negative index vulnerability. Verify whether any code path can set msg_size[idx] to 0 for a valid (positive) index, which would enable unlimited heap write without needing the negative index.\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"cookie_size is read via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and checked with 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle' (signed <= 256). calloc(cookie_size, 1) allocates exactly cookie_size bytes. read(0, buf, cookie_size) reads at most cookie_size bytes. The size check and allocation use the same value with no mismatch.\",\"reason\":\"The size check (<=256) and allocation (calloc(size,1)) and read (read(0,buf,size)) all use the same cookie_size value. No integer overflow possible since max is 256. No buffer overflow since read size equals allocation size. The check uses jle (signed) but cookie_size is expected to be positive, and negative values would cause calloc to fail (returning NULL) which would crash on read, not create an exploitable condition.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"menu_choice is read via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and dispatched via switch statement checking values 1-5.\",\"reason\":\"The switch statement only dispatches to functions for choices 1-5. Invalid choices fall through to default which loops back to menu. No memory operations performed on invalid input.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the write to FILE structure via edit_cookie negative index can corrupt function pointers within the FILE structure (e.g., __read_ptr, __write_ptr, __seek_ptr vtable pointers)\",\"reason\":\"The FILE structure layout depends on glibc version (2.39). The exact offset of function pointers within the FILE structure needs to be verified against the specific glibc version.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Analyze the _IO_FILE structure layout in glibc 2.39 to determine which fields are overwritten by read() at offset 0 and whether function pointers or buffer pointers are affected.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the probabilistic UAF in eat_cookie can be reliably triggered within the 60-second alarm timeout\",\"reason\":\"The random index from /dev/urandom makes the UAF probabilistic. The probability of hitting random_index == cookie_num-1 depends on cookie_num, and multiple eat operations may be needed.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Calculate the probability of triggering the self-assignment UAF condition and determine if it is feasible within the time constraint.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the duplicate pointer scenario in eat_cookie can lead to a double-free\",\"reason\":\"When random_index != cookie_num-1, a duplicate pointer is created. If eat_cookie later selects the other copy and frees it, a double-free occurs. The probability depends on the random index selection and the number of eat operations.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Analyze the probability of selecting the duplicate pointer in subsequent eat_cookie calls and verify whether glibc 2.39's tcache double-free detection would prevent exploitation.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":4,\"verified\":4,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in index check: edit_cookie and read_cookie use signed comparison (jl) for cookie_index < cookie_num. Negative indices pass the check because negative < positive in signed arithmetic. No lower-bound check (index >= 0) exists.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with negative index reads msg[cookie_index] from BSS before the msg[] array, reaching FILE* pointers (stdout/stdin/stderr) stored at lower BSS addresses. The FILE* value is passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\") which prints bytes from the FILE structure until NUL, leaking libc internal memory contents.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in index check\",\"read_cookie: negative index passes signed jl check\",\"msg[-4] reads stderr FILE* pointer (0x4040) from BSS\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", FILE_struct_ptr) prints FILE structure bytes until NUL\",\"libc FILE structure _flags field (0xfbad2087) and internal pointers leaked\",\"INFO_LEAK\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Index value (negative) selecting which FILE* pointer to dereference; leaked data is FILE structure contents at fixed libc address\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends negative cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") in read_cookie (menu option 4)\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"cookie_index must be negative to pass signed jl check (negative < cookie_num in signed comparison)\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without negative index, the check blocks OOB access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[negative_index] must contain a valid pointer (FILE* pointer at BSS 0x4020-0x4040)\",\"necessity\":\"Required - NULL pointer would cause crash instead of leak\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The FILE structure at the pointer address must contain non-NUL bytes before the first NUL for meaningful leak\",\"necessity\":\"Required - NUL at offset 0 would leak nothing\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(0x4060 - 32) = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE*\",\"alias\":\"BSS stderr@@GLIBC_2.2.5 at 0x4040 aliases to msg[-4]\",\"implication\":\"Index -4 in read_cookie dereferences stderr FILE* and leaks FILE structure contents via printf %s\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-6] = *(0x4060 - 48) = *(0x4030) = stdin FILE*\",\"alias\":\"BSS stdin@@GLIBC_2.2.5 at 0x4030 aliases to msg[-6]\",\"implication\":\"Index -6 in read_cookie dereferences stdin FILE* and leaks FILE structure contents\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060 - 64) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*\",\"alias\":\"BSS stdout@@GLIBC_2.2.5 at 0x4020 aliases to msg[-8]\",\"implication\":\"Index -8 in read_cookie dereferences stdout FILE* and leaks FILE structure contents\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie (menu option 4) with negative index -4, capture printf output containing FILE structure bytes\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86-64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked bytes matching FILE struct _flags field (0xfbad2087 low bytes: 87 20 ad fb)\",\"Non-zero bytes from libc FILE structure memory\",\"Multiple different leaks from different FILE structures (stderr/stdin/stdout)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base address but does not affect the relative BSS layout. FILE* pointers in BSS still alias to negative msg[] indices. The leaked FILE structure addresses reveal libc base, enabling ASLR bypass.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only after relocation. Does not affect INFO_LEAK primitive which reads FILE structure contents, not GOT.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution on stack/heap. Does not affect INFO_LEAK primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary protects return addresses. Does not affect INFO_LEAK primitive which reads from FILE structures in libc.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Index check: 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 17bb' uses signed comparison jl. cookie_index (rdx) from scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") as signed int64. No lower-bound check. Negative values pass because -N < cookie_num in signed arithmetic.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"Data flow: msg[cookie_index] read at 0x17bb (lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,msg; mov rax,[rdx+rax]), then printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) at 0x17e1. Negative index accesses BSS before msg array, reaching FILE* pointers.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060-0x4020\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[32] at 0x4060, stdout at 0x4020, stdin at 0x4030, stderr at 0x4040. msg[-4]=*(0x4040)=stderr FILE*, msg[-6]=*(0x4030)=stdin FILE*, msg[-8]=*(0x4020)=stdout FILE*. These are valid libc pointers dereferenced by printf.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"Runtime output with index=-4: printf(\\\"%s\\\", stderr_FILE_ptr) printed bytes 0x87 0x20 0xad 0xfb from the FILE structure, matching _flags field 0xfbad2087 (little-endian: 87 20 ad fb). Confirmed information leak of libc memory contents.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC verification: read_cookie with indices -4, -6, -8 all produced non-zero leaked bytes from 3 different FILE structures (stderr, stdin, stdout), confirming RELATIVE_READ from multiple fixed BSS targets.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2087 (stderr _flags, leaked bytes: 87 20 ad fb)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"read_cookie(-4) leaked 4 bytes (0x87 0x20 0xad 0xfb) from stderr FILE structure via printf %s. Bytes match _flags field 0xfbad2087. Also verified leaks from stdin (idx=-6) and stdout (idx=-8) FILE structures. PoC at /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py outputs '=== PRIM-001: VERIFIED ==='.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT primitive: negative index -> FILE* dereference -> printf %s leak. No intermediate steps. The leaked _flags field (0xfbad2087) is a known constant in glibc FILE structures, confirming the leak source. The FILE structure also contains libc pointers (buffer pointers at offset 8+) that can disclose libc base address for ASLR bypass, though they may be truncated by NUL bytes in the _flags field.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in index check: edit_cookie uses signed comparison (jl) for cookie_index < cookie_num. Negative indices pass the check. Combined with BUG-003 (integer underflow in size calculation: msg_size[idx]=0 -> 0-1=-1 -> sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), this produces an unlimited-size write to a fixed target.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with negative index reads msg[cookie_index] from BSS before msg[] array, reaching FILE* pointers. The FILE* value becomes the buffer argument to read(0, FILE_ptr, unlimited_size), writing attacker-controlled bytes from stdin directly into the FILE structure in libc writable memory.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in index check\",\"BUG-003: msg_size[negative_idx]=0 -> 0-1=-1 -> movsxd -> 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (unlimited size)\",\"edit_cookie: negative index passes signed jl check\",\"msg[-4] reads stderr FILE* pointer (0x4040) from BSS\",\"msg_size[-4] reads 0 from BSS padding -> size=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"read(0, stderr_FILE_ptr, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) writes attacker data to FILE struct\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target: FILE structure)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write content (attacker-controlled bytes from stdin via read()); write size (unlimited due to integer underflow); write target is FIXED to FILE structure address (determined by negative index, not attacker-controlled)\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends negative cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") in edit_cookie (menu option 3), then sends payload bytes via stdin which read() writes to FILE struct\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"cookie_index must be negative to pass signed jl check\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without negative index, the check blocks OOB access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[negative_index] must contain a valid writable pointer (FILE* pointer at BSS 0x4020-0x4040 pointing to libc FILE struct in rw memory)\",\"necessity\":\"Required - NULL pointer causes crash; read-only target causes EFAULT\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg_size[negative_index] must be 0 (or small) to produce unlimited (or large) write size via integer underflow\",\"necessity\":\"Required - normal msg_size values produce bounded writes\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"The FILE structure memory must be writable (libc .data/.bss segment is RW)\",\"necessity\":\"Required - read-only target would cause EFAULT\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"stdin data must be available for read() to consume after scanf consumes menu input\",\"necessity\":\"Required - if stdin is exhausted, read() returns -1 and no write occurs\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE* -> _IO_2_1_stderr_ at 0x7ffff7fa74e0\",\"alias\":\"BSS stderr@@GLIBC_2.2.5 at 0x4040 aliases to msg[-4]\",\"implication\":\"Index -4 in edit_cookie writes to stderr FILE structure in libc RW memory\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-6] = *(0x4030) = stdin FILE* -> _IO_2_1_stdin_ at 0x7ffff7fa68e0\",\"alias\":\"BSS stdin@@GLIBC_2.2.5 at 0x4030 aliases to msg[-6]\",\"implication\":\"Index -6 in edit_cookie writes to stdin FILE structure in libc RW memory\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* -> _IO_2_1_stdout_ at 0x7ffff7fa75c0\",\"alias\":\"BSS stdout@@GLIBC_2.2.5 at 0x4020 aliases to msg[-8]\",\"implication\":\"Index -8 in edit_cookie writes to stdout FILE structure in libc RW memory\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-4] = *(0x4150) = 0 (BSS padding, high bytes of msg[30])\",\"alias\":\"msg_size[-4] at 0x4150 aliases to high 4 bytes of msg[30] (NULL)\",\"implication\":\"Size = 0 - 1 = -1, sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (unlimited write). All negative msg_size indices read 0 from BSS padding.\"},{\"expression\":\"Two-step write ARB_WRITE assessment: msg[] entries set by create_cookie (calloc result), eat_cookie (pointer swap), init (strdup result) - none allow attacker to place arbitrary address in msg[] slot\",\"alias\":\"No alias path to control msg[] entry values\",\"implication\":\"Two-step write to ARB_WRITE NOT feasible: attacker cannot set msg[idx] to arbitrary address Y. Write target remains fixed to FILE struct addresses. Primitive is RELATIVE_WRITE, not ARB_WRITE.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"GDB breakpoint at read() call in edit_cookie with index=-4, verify rsi=FILE struct address and rdx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, then use GDB call read(0, target, 16) to write and verify FILE struct modification\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86-64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"GDB: rsi = stderr FILE struct address (0x7ffff7fa74e0)\",\"GDB: rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (unlimited size)\",\"GDB: FILE struct before write = 0x00000000fbad2087\",\"GDB: FILE struct after write = 0x4242424241414141 (attacker data)\",\"GDB: same verification for stdin FILE struct (index -6)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base but BSS layout is preserved. FILE* pointers still alias to negative msg[] indices. The write target (FILE struct in libc) is at a fixed offset from libc base.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only. Does not prevent write to FILE structures in libc .data/.bss segment (which is RW). GOT overwrite is not the attack vector here.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution on stack/heap. Does not prevent data writes to FILE structures.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary protects return addresses. Does not affect write to FILE structures in libc.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Index check: 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 1692' uses signed comparison jl. No lower-bound check. Negative values pass.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16de\",\"description\":\"Data flow: msg_size[cookie_index] read at 0x16a7, sub eax,1 at 0x16b9 (underflow if 0), movsxd rdx,eax at 0x